  "Number": 14,
  "Question": "Which of the following integers cannot be written as the sum of four consecutive odd integers?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\( 16\\)",
    "B": "\\( 40\\)",
    "C": "\\( 72\\)",
    "D": "\\(100\\)",
    "E": "\\( 200 \\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Four consecutive odd integers are equally spaced. Their mean is the integer halfway between the middle two terms, because the outer two terms have that same midpoint. Between two consecutive odd integers lies an even integer, so this mean is even.\n\nThe sum is therefore four times an even integer, which must be a multiple of \\(8\\). But\n\\[\n100=8\\cdot12+4,\n\\]\nso \\(100\\) cannot be such a sum.\n\nTo check that every other choice is possible, we can exhibit the required sums:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n16&=1+3+5+7,\\\\\n40&=7+9+11+13,\\\\\n72&=15+17+19+21,\\\\\n200&=47+49+51+53.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus the integer that cannot be written in the required way is \\(\\boxed{100}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
